ry and  genetics  in my last year of training i had a  chance to work two months in a  hematology lab  and four months in the genetics lab  though i still loved surgery  i felt that the hematology lab was a  better suited career for me  and i enjoyed the work behind the  microscope combined with technical acts  such as bone marrow  aspirations and biopsies after my  five-year internship at the hazmat  university hospital of which three were  in hematology  i was hired there as a resident i  started teaching  laboratory hematology to the interns  who were training in our lab and also  taking on more responsibilities  then the head of our department asked me  to replace him for a five-day  residential seminar on leadership  this was a turning point for me it gave  me an interest in hr management  and a whole new perspective on my work  the hospital  and the way all the departments  interacted  then through a combination of  circumstances i took the head of the  cellular hematology lab  and reorganized its its activities and  manage the 10 lab technicians that  worked there  those were my first steps in management  after 17 years spent at the university  hospital and knowing that i didn't want  to pursue  an academic career i decided that it was  time to spread my ways  so i left the university environment for  a private hospital where i headed the  hematology lab  the hospital being small and the  activity reduced  it gave me time to explore new avenues  for my career  i became the president of scientific  society namely the belgian society for  advancement of cytometry  and completed a master's degree in  hospital management in evening classes  after 20 years of frontline hematology  work and long conversations with  my husband whose area of expertise is  administration  and hr management i realized that i was  ready to take a step back  while using all the experience i had  accumulated in the field of laboratory  medicine  that opportunity arose in the shape of  becoming the head of the laboratory  of a big private hospital of 1100 beds  to sum up this more personal part of the  talk i would say that what triggered my  career change was the desire to become  an active part of the decision making  process in every aspect of the lab and  apply logic  based on objective elements for its  management  i also strongly believe that what makes  the wealth of any business  is its human resources we therefore need  to provide training and instill  motivation to the staff  in this context i strongly recommend  this wonderful training tool  for hematology technicians and  clinicians alike  and invite you to take a look at this  hemet image program  i would like to emphasize that i don't  have any commercial interest  in this program  in order to infer the key qualities and  training needed to become a lab manager  we need to understand the role of the  clinical lab and that of the clinical  this is best described by the mayo  clinic they estimate that 60 to 70  percent  of all decisions regarding as patients  care and management  are based on laboratory test results  this also highlights the role of the  clinical biologist  which is crucial to improve the  laboratory in clinical interface  indeed the clinical biologists know the  circumstances in which the tests were  conducted from the acquisition of the  sample  until the send out of the results if  something along the way happened that  could have altered  the results it is a responsibility of  the clinical biologist to interpret  these results  so that the clinician would not jump to  the wrong conclusion  i believe that medicine is so vast and  so complex that it's not possible to be  omniscient  therefore it is a clinical biologist's  role to guide test utilization  in the fast-moving industry that is  diagnostic test development  it is a clinical biologist's job to stay  up to date  and provide physicians with the  information regarding the  characteristics  and the discriminatory power of  available tests  the same way that the physicians are  expected to build their clinical  approach on evidence-based medicine  the clinical biologists must visit their  approach on evidence-based laboratory  medicine  together with the physicians they must  help clarify the questions asked  in order to propose a relevant test best  suited to bring pertinent answers  only in this way leaving ego aside will  we effectively use the limited resources  available in healthcare  thus the mission of the laboratory is to  support the health care system through  providing fast  reliable and high quality results  offering state-of-the-art  testing in accordance with  evidence-based laboratory medicine  delivering medical support for adequate  detection diagnosis  and treatment of diseases as well as  disease prevention  and i will come back to that later and  due to the way  hospitals are families in belgium its  additional essential role  is to compensate for the hospital's  chronic underfunding  knowing all this i would like to share  my thoughts on what is needed to  successfully achieve good laboratory  management  as you've seen my journey was long and  gave me time to know myself  you may be faster than me but it will  still take time and you won't become a  manager overnight  it's important to examine your  motivation before taking on this  challenge and i think that you will have  a better chance of  chance of success if your main objective  is to do things better according to the  vision you have for the lab  for its workers and for its clients  their success and well-being will be  your success  and well-being it's paramount to know  what i call your playground rules  which will guide your choices and  decisions  for instance know the local rules and  regulations for the lab  for hr management and for equipment  acquisition  learn how the lab is financed and if i  have one piece of advice to give it  would be  always come prepared to meetings if you  don't  it's a waste of time and energy be  present  don't look at your phone listen don't  lose sight of  your objectives and don't be afraid to  act to ask questions  and if your questions have not been  answered don't make a decision so long  as things remain unclear  so now that you're the lab manager you  must define your vision  meaning the objectives you aim to reach  in my case  the aim for my lab is to become a lab of  excellence  iso 15 a189 certified  offering a wide very wide range of  relevant tests in order to control the  quality and speed  of the results transmitted to the  clinicians all consistent with the new  funding measures in the sector  in order to ensure its financial  profitability  define in your vision will give you the  guiding thread you need to follow  in all your projects  the lab is a major element of the  hospital and its organization  must be consistent with the organization  of the rest of the hospital  here i share with you the five pillars  that have been chosen in my hospital  and that are the same in all the care  polls  each poll defines its key performance  indicator  within each pillar it is your job as a  lab manager  to think about what you aim at for your  patients and for your prescribers which  are also your clients  you must also define your economical  objectives  and how you're going to reach them with  what process and what resources  and then you have to be able to measure  the effectiveness of everything you've  put into place  those will be the objective elements  that will guide your decision-making  process  so what are the carefuls i've been  talking about that define the hospitals  organizations  the care polls are functional units  grouping doctors and hospital  practitioners working together  they represent a significant volume of  activity  and are dedicated to a homogeneous group  of patients  for example geriatric patients or  a homogeneous group of pathologies for  example cancer  so they can be made up of one