try  after having been  subsidized for the university or  graduate studies  our system relying heavily on our  workforce becomes unbalanced  thus if nothing changes in our  healthcare fund  funding model it is imperative to  maintain the financial sustainability of  our healthcare system through efficiency  and effectiveness strategies we all  believe that our health is priceless  but it has a cost  i would now like to go more into the  details of the lab's financing system  today's system is the result of trials  and error and further fine-tuning  if all the financing were to be based  exclusively on itemization  the temptation would be strong for the  hospital to maximize  profits through pushing the physicians  to massively prescribe  tests thus increasing the burden on  health care expenditure  on the other hand if all the financing  were based exclusively on a flat rate  the temptation would be strong to  minimize test prescription  to maximize income while minimizing  expenses  but this attitude would be detrimental  to patient care  as already mentioned belgium is a  country of compromise  the current system combines 75 of  reimbursement as a flat rate  and 25 based on itemization  the limits uh abuse further  reimbursement is restricted by about 150  accumulation rules  for example if a resource site  sedimentation rate  and fibrino fibrinogen are prescribed  together  only one will be reimbursed and there's  also  about 90 diagnostic rules for example  homocysteine will only be reimbursed for  patients age less than  55 years old with clinical evidence of  vascular pathology  this implies an important responsibility  of the prescriber to provide clinical  information although this is rarely the  case  more recently no new flat rates were  introduced  for low variability care for example  there is one flat rate for the  for hip replacement and the distribution  of the medical  fees among the specialists involved in  the patient's care is determined by the  national institute for health care  and disability insurance the problem  is that fewer and fewer recent tests are  reimbursed  and thus the financial burden is being  transferred to the patients  this jeopardizes healthcare  accessibility one of the fundamental  principles of  our social security system  and to complicate things further because  as our local saying goes  why make things simple when you can make  them complicated  we have additional rules for  hospitalized patients  there are two more flat rates paid to  the lab for each patient admission and  one another one for each hospitalization  day  and this one based on the case mix of  the hospital  the annual bed shot for clinical biology  for clinical biology last year  was close to 1.5 billion euros thus  namely 5  of the overall health care budget  in the context of market changes and to  keep the system viable  healthcare institutions and laboratories  must adapt  this has led to the reorganization of  the hospital landscape  into networks facilitated by  digitalization to share patient data  the information and communication  technologies should prevent the  duplication of tests and services  but as mentioned before this evolution  must be accompanied by reforms  in the financing working groups  designated by the authorities have been  tasked  with readjusting the nomenclature for  medical care in general and for clinical  biology in particular  one of the main projects is to find  financial incentives  to switch from a sick model to a  prevention model  the rationale being that the longer  people remain healthy  the less financial burden they will be  on the health care system  we see today with the kovic pandemic how  hard it is to convince the population to  take adequate  preventive measures and namely  vaccinations  which would greatly release pressure on  every country's health care systems  people have increased expectations of  science and have forgotten that before  massive vaccination programs around the  world  many people died of infectious diseases  i could go on for hours but this is not  the point of today's talk  but it illustrates the importance of  prevention in any health care system  and its cost effectiveness another way  to increase cost effectiveness  is to integrate the rational use of  resources  and quality in our fee-for-service  system  and lastly the current trend in  laboratory medicine  is to consolidate multiple labs into a  single one within a network  of hospitals  i would like to discuss with you this  last idea and review its pros and cons  as i said in the beginning my talk is  centered around  hospital labs and it's important to keep  that in mind for this discussion  we all know that the ideas behind the  consolidation is to increase the  critical mass of tests  to go past a break-even point and  increase profitability  the theory is that we will achieve a  decreased cost per test  thanks to an increased turnover a  rationalization of hr  and economies of scale that business  model might be adequate for private labs  that don't have the constraints of  hospital  labs in our setting consolidation  implies the multiplication  of small emergency labs because  hospitals treat patients 24  7. the possible benefits of economies of  skill  would have to be reinvested in triage  transport  and support of the emergency labs with  the consequence of a lower specimen  quality  due to transport logistical difficulties  and longer turnaround times redundancy  of testing platforms are essential in a  hospital lab  as is optimal communication between  sectors for example  if we receive a limited amount of  cerebral spinal fluid  the same tube will go first to  microbiology then hematology and finally  to chemistry in the same way  a direct contact between hematology and  the blood bank  will speed up the bleeding patients  management  and we need additional human resources  to cover  this time slots outside working hours  i would thus propose an alternate model  in the hospital setting  i believe that each hospital of the  network should keep its  in-house lab with all the main routine  activities  which include all the emergency tests  this would allow the preservation of the  best possible turnaround time  and service to the hospital clinicians  with the expertise of the clinical  biologists remaining in the hospital  indeed the bulk of the cost is not due  to the size of the analyzers  but is mainly due to the region to the  reagents  consumables and disposables disposables  which are all activity related  and human resources who are already  present 24  7. i think that the hospitals in the  network  should agree to mutualize specialized  non-urgent tests in a single site  this is where consolidation is the most  cost effective  without affecting quality or service i'm  thinking of activities such as  autoimmunity allergology mass  spectrometry  etc  and this brings us to the end of this  talk i would like to conclude with the  world health organization's definition  of the continuum of care  which is the offer of a complete service  array from hospitals to home care  and which requires all medical and  social services within the community  to be brought together laboratory  medicine plays a central role in this  continuum of care  and i think i demonstrated that our  social security  security in belgium aims at offering  this continuum of care despite new  funding challenges  but in order for this continuum of care  to be a reality  the patient has to stand in the center  of the healthcare supply chain  and for all the healthcare initiatives  to converge towards them  this implies that for every patient and  for every type of pathology  the most adequate and available  treatment should be suggested  regardless of profitability for any  level  or actor the most important selection  criterion  must be the patient's needs  i prepared this slide for further  reading and to give you some food for  thought  thank you for your attention and i will  be happy to answer your questions  thank you dr cornreich for your  informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have a question you would like to  ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen we'll answer as many  of your questions  as we have time for okay let's get  started  our first question is  does it mean that there are no medical  laboratory scientists working with the  care poll  yes that's right the way the  laboratories are organized in belgium  the there are no medical laboratory  scientists working  they're replaced by the clinical  biologist which  uh has either a medical degree  or pharma pharmacist degree and the  specialty takes uh  five years of master study uh to get to  that level after having done  either medical school bingham becoming a  doctor  or uh becoming a pharmacist uh then  in some university hospital labs there  may be some  uh medical laboratory scientists but  usually there  there aren't any  thank you dr cornright okay our next  question  is is the idea of the care polls a  standard in belgium  or is this an innovative program that  your institution has created and  implemented  implemented okay a care portals are not  a  novel idea other hospitals have them too  but never in such an extensive way as we  did in our hospital  sometimes they just combine a few  specialties together and call them poles  but in our hospital all medical  specialties  are regrouped in distinct care pools  and like i said we have 13 in our  hospital  and so this is really something  innovative because there are no more  medical departments that are independent  of the care pool  thank you again dr kornwreck our next  question  is in an ideal world how do you envision  the laboratory clinician partnership  what do you think is necessary to reach  this ideal  this is a very good question and  unfortunately we don