ry and  genetics  in my last year of training i had a  chance to work two months in a  hematology lab  and four months in the genetics lab  though i still loved surgery  i felt that the hematology lab was a  better suited career for me  and i enjoyed the work behind the  microscope combined with technical acts  such as bone marrow  aspirations and biopsies after my  five-year internship at the hazmat  university hospital of which three were  in hematology  i was hired there as a resident i  started teaching  laboratory hematology to the interns  who were training in our lab and also  taking on more responsibilities  then the head of our department asked me  to replace him for a five-day  residential seminar on leadership  this was a turning point for me it gave  me an interest in hr management  and a whole new perspective on my work  the hospital  and the way all the departments  interacted  then through a combination of  circumstances i took the head of the  cellular hematology lab  and reorganized its its activities and  manage the 10 lab technicians that  worked there  those were my first steps in management  after 17 years spent at the university  hospital and knowing that i didn't want  to pursue  an academic career i decided that it was  time to spread my ways  so i left the university environment for  a private hospital where i headed the  hematology lab  the hospital being small and the  activity reduced  it gave me time to explore new avenues  for my career  i became the president of scientific  society namely the belgian society for  advancement of cytometry  and completed a master's degree in  hospital management in evening classes  after 20 years of frontline hematology  work and long conversations with  my husband whose area of expertise is  administration  and hr management i realized that i was  ready to take a step back  while using all the experience i had  accumulated in the field of laboratory  medicine  that opportunity arose in the shape of  becoming the head of the laboratory  of a big private hospital of 1100 beds  to sum up this more personal part of the  talk i would say that what triggered my  career change was the desire to become  an active part of the decision making  process in every aspect of the lab and  apply logic  based on objective elements for its  management  i also strongly believe that what makes  the wealth of any business  is its human resources we therefore need  to provide training and instill  motivation to the staff  in this context i strongly recommend  this wonderful training tool  for hematology technicians and  clinicians alike  and invite you to take a look at this  hemet image program  i would like to emphasize that i don't  have any commercial interest  in this program  in order to infer the key qualities and  training needed to become a lab manager  we need to understand the role of the  clinical lab and that of the clinical  this is best described by the mayo  clinic they estimate that 60 to 70  percent  of all decisions regarding as patients  care and management  are based on laboratory test results  this also highlights the role of the  clinical biologist  which is crucial to improve the  laboratory in clinical interface  indeed the clinical biologists know the  circumstances in which the tests were  conducted from the acquisition of the  sample  until the send out of the results if  something along the way happened that  could have altered  the results it is a responsibility of  the clinical biologist to interpret  these results  so that the clinician would not jump to  the wrong conclusion  i believe that medicine is so vast and  so complex that it's not possible to be  omniscient  therefore it is a clinical biologist's  role to guide test utilization  in the fast-moving industry that is  diagnostic test development  it is a clinical biologist's job to stay  up to date  and provide physicians with the  information regarding the  characteristics  and the discriminatory power of  available tests  the same way that the physicians are  expected to build their clinical  approach on evidence-based medicine  the clinical biologists must visit their  approach on evidence-based laboratory  medicine  together with the physicians they must  help clarify the questions asked  in order to propose a relevant test best  suited to bring pertinent answers  only in this way leaving ego aside will  we effectively use the limited resources  available in healthcare  thus the mission of the laboratory is to  support the health care system through  providing fast  reliable and high quality results  offering state-of-the-art  testing in accordance with  evidence-based laboratory medicine  delivering medical support for adequate  detection diagnosis  and treatment of diseases as well as  disease prevention  and i will come back to that later and  due to the way  hospitals are families in belgium its  additional essential role  is to compensate for the hospital's  chronic underfunding  knowing all this i would like to share  my thoughts on what is needed to  successfully achieve good laboratory  management  as you've seen my journey was long and  gave me time to know myself  you may be faster than me but it will  still take time and you won't become a  manager overnight  it's important to examine your  motivation before taking on this  challenge and i think that you will have  a better chance of  chance of success if your main objective  is to do things better according to the  vision you have for the lab  for its workers and for its clients  their success and well-being will be  your success  and well-being it's paramount to know  what i call your playground rules  which will guide your choices and  decisions  for instance know the local rules and  regulations for the lab  for hr management and for equipment  acquisition  learn how the lab is financed and if i  have one piece of advice to give it  would be  always come prepared to meetings if you  don't  it's a waste of time and energy be  present  don't look at your phone listen don't  lose sight of  your objectives and don't be afraid to  act to ask questions  and if your questions have not been  answered don't make a decision so long  as things remain unclear  so now that you're the lab manager you  must define your vision  meaning the objectives you aim to reach  in my case  the aim for my lab is to become a lab of  excellence  iso 15 a189 certified  offering a wide very wide range of  relevant tests in order to control the  quality and speed  of the results transmitted to the  clinicians all consistent with the new  funding measures in the sector  in order to ensure its financial  profitability  define in your vision will give you the  guiding thread you need to follow  in all your projects  the lab is a major element of the  hospital and its organization  must be consistent with the organization  of the rest of the hospital  here i share with you the five pillars  that have been chosen in my hospital  and that are the same in all the care  polls  each poll defines its key performance  indicator  within each pillar it is your job as a  lab manager  to think about what you aim at for your  patients and for your prescribers which  are also your clients  you must also define your economical  objectives  and how you're going to reach them with  what process and what resources  and then you have to be able to measure  the effectiveness of everything you've  put into place  those will be the objective elements  that will guide your decision-making  process  so what are the carefuls i've been  talking about that define the hospitals  organizations  the care polls are functional units  grouping doctors and hospital  practitioners working together  they represent a significant volume of  activity  and are dedicated to a homogeneous group  of patients  for example geriatric patients or  a homogeneous group of pathologies for  example cancer  so they can be made up of one or several  departments  whether medical or surgical or both  in my hospital there are 10 clinical  care polls centered on systems  and three support care polls which are  the lab  the imagery department and the acute  care medicine department  the hospital's medical director and the  13 care pool heads  constitute a committee that meets every  two weeks to  discuss transversal projects  additionally this committee joined by  the directors committee  constitute the strategic committee of  the hospital  which meets twice a year and defines the  objectives for the hospital  and the necessary means to attain them  for example the decision to aim for the  accreditation of the hospital was taken  by the strategic committee  so what drove the reorganization of the  hospital in care polls  well mainly the evolution of medicine  and healthcare policies from individual  medicine to comprehensive health care  this requires multidisciplinarity in the  management of the patients  and therefore the idea was to gather a  set of clinical functions  to achieve that in an efficient and  harmonized way  and thus everyone reaps the benefits of  it of this organization  the patients through coordinated and  organized teams to take care of them  in a multidisciplinary approach the care  teams that can combine their resources  and skills and share their expertise  they have a more holistic view of their  patients and transversality is  facilitated  because there's a single point of  contact for each pole with a power of  decision  and finally the general practitioners  find satisfaction in this system  because their patients are assured the  best skills in the same entity  with multiple entry points for the same  pathology  for example for a herniated disc it's  the same pole  and the same coherent care path for also  an euro  so i want to share with you how our care  pose is structured  the lab is headed by either a medical  doctor or pharmacist  with a master's degree in a laboratory  medicine which we call a clinical  biologist  other clinical biologists with specific  competence in biochemistry  hematology or microbiology work in their  respective sectors  all are independent contractors thus  only have a functional authority with  the team managers  and the lab technicians working in their  respective sectors  together with the team managers the  clinical biologist organizes the  sector's activities and develop  and define the tests that are to be used  the hierarchical authority is exercised  by the labs executive manager  for the administrative aspects of hr  management  and by the scientific manager in  collaboration with the clinical  biologist for the scientific validation  of the test  all the lab activities are supported by  ancillary functions  such as quality assurance i.t and  various  administrative tasks the financial  engineer is provided by the financial  department of the hospital and supplies  financial data  on the lab's activities this allows  management based on objective and  measurable indicators  the pulse medical head the executive  manager  and the financial engineer constitute  the polls management trial  and that brings us to the financial  aspects of the job and understanding the  constraints we must face  the basic concept that underlies our  social security system in belgium is  solidarity  this solidarity is guaranteed because  all working people must contribute  to the system based on their salary and  all citizens contribute through this  taxes  thus the funding is provided by three  sources the state  the employers and the workers more  recently  a fourth source of financing is a  percentage of vat receipts and  additional excise duties  the national social security office  collects all those revenues  and distributes them among the seven  branches of social security  among those seven branches is the  compulsory insurance for all belgian  citizens for medical care and benefits  its budget was over 27 billion euros  last year  as everything in belgium all decisions  on the various aspects of the system are  the result of a compromise  in this case between the trade unions  the mutual insurance funds and the  employers organizations  the elementary pillars of our healthcare  philosophy  are quality and accessibility the  principle of free choice is paramount  this means that the patients can freely  choose their doctor  and the doctor can freely choose their  patients except in case of emergency  and has diagnostic and therapeutic  freedom  of course this has a cost and therefore  the key challenge  will be increasingly to provide health  care that is both affordable  and accessible while still maintaining  high quality  i would like to discuss with you the  various factors that threaten our  current model of healthcare financing  the aging of the populations concerns  baby boomers in our industrialized  countries  it means that the actual workforce  contributing to the social security  is less numerous than the population  they're mostly contributing  for and which are the biggest consumers  of healthcare  we also face an exponential growth of  healthcare demand  associated with new technological and  therapeutic developments  leading to drastic increase in  healthcare expenditure  also when health care becomes  digitalized and automated  new forms of pension arise in the health  care demand  and remuneration systems reforms are  necessary to ensure that the benefits of  digitalization  and automation through its network and  scale effects  will come into their own and finally  globalization has unforeseen effects  although freedom of movement is  undoubtedly a good thing  it allows our youth to seek work in  other countries  as shown in a previous slide our social  security covers all aspects of life  from birth to death as long as our  population  remains in belgium the beneficiaries of  our social security are also its  contributors  but when our youth leaves the country  after having been  subsidized for the university or  graduate studies  our system relying heavily on our  workforce becomes unbalanced  thus if nothing changes in our  healthcare fund  funding model it is imperative to  maintain the financial sustainability of  our healthcare system through efficiency  and effectiveness strategies we all  believe that our health is priceless  but it has a cost  i would now like to go more into the  details of the lab's financing system  today's system is the result of trials  and error and further fine-tuning  if all the financing were to be based  exclusively on itemization  the temptation would be strong for the  hospital to maximize  profits through pushing the physicians  to massively prescribe  tests thus increasing the burden on  health care expenditure  on the other hand if all the financing  were based exclusively on a flat rate  the temptation would be strong to  minimize test prescription  to maximize income while minimizing  expenses  but this attitude would be detrimental  to patient care  as already mentioned belgium is a  country of compromise  the current system combines 75 of  reimbursement as a flat rate  and 25 based on itemization  the limits uh abuse further  reimbursement is restricted by about 150  accumulation rules  for example if a resource site  sedimentation rate  and fibrino fibrinogen are prescribed  together  only one will be reimbursed and there's  also  about 90 diagnostic rules for example  homocysteine will only be reimbursed for  patients age less than  55 years old with clinical evidence of  vascular pathology  this implies an important responsibility  of the prescriber to provide clinical  information although this is rarely the  case  more recently no new flat rates were  introduced  for low variability care for example  there is one flat rate for the  for hip replacement and the distribution  of the medical  fees among the specialists involved in  the patient's care is determined by the  national institute for health care  and disability insurance the problem  is that fewer and fewer recent tests are  reimbursed  and thus the financial burden is being  transferred to the patients  this jeopardizes healthcare  accessibility one of the fundamental  principles of  our social security system  and to complicate things further because  as our local saying goes  why make things simple when you can make  them complicated  we have additional rules for  hospitalized patients  there are two more flat rates paid to  the lab for each patient admission and  one another one for each hospitalization  day  and this one based on the case mix of  the hospital  the annual bed shot for clinical biology  for clinical biology last year  was close to 1.5 billion euros thus  namely 5  of the overall health care budget  in the context of market changes and to  keep the system viable  healthcare institutions and laboratories  must adapt  this has led to the reorganization of  the hospital landscape  into networks facilitated by  digitalization to share patient data  the information and communication  technologies should prevent the  duplication of tests and services  but as mentioned before this evolution  must be accompanied by reforms  in the financing working groups  designated by the authorities have been  tasked  with readjusting the nomenclature for  medical care in general and for clinical  biology in particular  one of the main projects is to find  financial incentives  to switch from a sick model to a  prevention model  the rationale being that the longer  people remain healthy  the less financial burden they will be  on the health care system  we see today with the kovic pandemic how  hard it is to convince the population to  take adequate  preventive measures and namely  vaccinations  which would greatly release pressure on  every country's health care systems  people have increased expectations of  science and have forgotten that before  massive vaccination programs around the  world  many people died of infectious diseases  i could go on for hours but this is not  the point of today's talk  but it illustrates the importance of  prevention in any health care system  and its cost effectiveness another way  to increase cost effectiveness  is to integrate the rational use of  resources  and quality in our fee-for-service  system  and lastly the current trend in  laboratory medicine  is to consolidate multiple labs into a  single one within a network  of hospitals  i would like to discuss with you this  last idea and review its pros and cons  as i said in the beginning my talk is  centered around  hospital labs and it's important to keep  that in mind for this discussion  we all know that the ideas behind the  consolidation is to increase the  critical mass of tests  to go past a break-even point and  increase profitability  the theory is that we will achieve a  decreased cost per test  thanks to an increased turnover a  rationalization of hr  and economies of scale that business  model might be adequate for private labs  that don't have the constraints of  hospital  labs in our setting consolidation  implies the multiplication  of small emergency labs because  hospitals treat patients 24  7. the possible benefits of economies of  skill  would have to be reinvested in triage  transport  and support of the emergency labs with  the consequence of a lower specimen  quality  due to transport logistical difficulties  and longer turnaround times redundancy  of testing platforms are essential in a  hospital lab  as is optimal communication between  sectors for example  if we receive a limited amount of  cerebral spinal fluid  the same tube will go first to  microbiology then hematology and finally  to chemistry in the same way  a direct contact between hematology and  the blood bank  will speed up the bleeding patients  management  and we need additional human resources  to cover  this time slots outside working hours  i would thus propose an alternate model  in the hospital setting  i believe that each hospital of the  network should keep its  in-house lab with all the main routine  activities  which include all the emergency tests  this would allow the preservation of the  best possible turnaround time  and service to the hospital clinicians  with the expertise of the clinical  biologists remaining in the hospital  indeed the bulk of the cost is not due  to the size of the analyzers  but is mainly due to the region to the  reagents  consumables and disposables disposables  which are all activity related  and human resources who are already  present 24  7. i think that the hospitals in the  network  should agree to mutualize specialized  non-urgent tests in a single site  this is where consolidation is the most  cost effective  without affecting quality or service i'm  thinking of activities such as  autoimmunity allergology mass  spectrometry  etc  and this brings us to the end of this  talk i would like to conclude with the  world health organization's definition  of the continuum of care  which is the offer of a complete service  array from hospitals to home care  and which requires all medical and  social services within the community  to be brought together laboratory  medicine plays a central role in this  continuum of care  and i think i demonstrated that our  social security  security in belgium aims at offering  this continuum of care despite new  funding challenges  but in order for this continuum of care  to be a reality  the patient has to stand in the center  of the healthcare supply chain  and for all the healthcare initiatives  to converge towards them  this implies that for every patient and  for every type of pathology  the most adequate and available  treatment should be suggested  regardless of profitability for any  level  or actor the most important selection  criterion  must be the patient's needs  i prepared this slide for further  reading and to give you some food for  thought  thank you for your attention and i will  be happy to answer your questions  thank you dr cornreich for your  informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have a question you would like to  ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen we'll answer as many  of your questions  as we have time for okay let's get  started  our first question is  does it mean that there are no medical  laboratory scientists working with the  care poll  yes that's right the way the  laboratories are organized in belgium  the there are no medical laboratory  scientists working  they're replaced by the clinical  biologist which  uh has either a medical degree  or pharma pharmacist degree and the  specialty takes uh  five years of master study uh to get to  that level after having done  either medical school bingham becoming a  doctor  or uh becoming a pharmacist uh then  in some university hospital labs there  may be some  uh medical laboratory scientists but  usually there  there aren't any  thank you dr cornright okay our next  question  is is the idea of the care polls a  standard in belgium  or is this an innovative program that  your institution has created and  implemented  implemented okay a care portals are not  a  novel idea other hospitals have them too  but never in such an extensive way as we  did in our hospital  sometimes they just combine a few  specialties together and call them poles  but in our hospital all medical  specialties  are regrouped in distinct care pools  and like i said we have 13 in our  hospital  and so this is really something  innovative because there are no more  medical departments that are independent  of the care pool  thank you again dr kornwreck our next  question  is in an ideal world how do you envision  the laboratory clinician partnership  what do you think is necessary to reach  this ideal  this is a very good question and  unfortunately we don't live in an ideal  world  this is why also in belgium and in other  european countries  we have this specialty  of laboratory medicine for doctors and  pharmacists  so that the clinician  can discuss cases  equal to equal with uh the laboratory  doctor or pharmacist because we had  the same training and we went through  this  um also uh clinical  training before becoming laboratory  doctors and so  this allows the discussion to be on the  same level  so in the ideal world the clinician um  and this is what the authorities are  trying to finance  the clinicians have to ask the questions  okay i have this patient that has  this such and such uh symptoms and signs  uh how can the lab help me  get to the diagnosis uh in the best  way use the best test available  how do i interpret the results what are  the possible  interferences that we'll have with uh  those tests those  would be the ideal way of working and  the most  uh cost effective way also  all right thank you again uh we've got  another question  about the care polls uh was there um  were there any barriers to the  implementation of these care polls  no they there wasn't there weren't any  barriers this was  discussed at length with uh  ambassadors so we had a strategic  meeting  with ambassadors from  each uh uh  poll that that that we thought were  going to come into existence  so in the ambassador for each for was  this uh designated  by all the departments that were  supposed to be in that  poll and those ambassadors uh  participated in the first strategic  meeting with the  directors committee and that allowed to  um to determine  which poles were going to come into  um into being  and uh what were expected of the careful  heads  and so the ambassadors went back to the  different departments that were that  were included in their polls  and then elections were done in each  poll to elect  the polls head the the only  difference is that when a poll  encompasses several departments  then if there was an election but when a  poll  is a unique department like for example  the laboratory poll  the head of the department was  automatically the head of the poll  okay great thanks again dr kornreich her  next question is  what did your previous structure look  like before care polls  and were there any problems with that  yes  so the previous structure were like  i think most hospitals we had  about 30 to 40 medical departments  for each medical specialty and  so the problem with that is that we  to to go forward  all together you have to and we had  meetings with all the head of  departments but that means having  meetings with a lot of people  and it's sort of it's it was sort of  chaotic and also um  the hospitals is organized in such a way  that we have bilateral meetings between  the head of the department  and the medical director  the general director and the financial  director of the hospital  twice a year where we would show  the annual financial financial  statements of  each department and discuss  future projects and investments that we  wanted to do  inside each head each department but  that meant also  for the directors to to meet with  uh twice a year with 30 to 40 different  different departments regrouping  everything in polls  first of all allowed to limit those  meetings  and to have already an idea inside the  poll  because things are already managed  inside each poll  including uh the the investments  so there was already an arbitrage inside  the poll  and then they were presented during  those bilateral meetings  twice a year uh to the directors for the  directors is also allowed  uh to have a general view of all the  medical activities in the hospital  um in a very short uh  and concentrated time and  to have uh to better guide  the the strategic aim of of the hospital  and of the medical projects  and like i said we also have meetings of  the  head for the paul hebs committee um  twice a month which also allows us to  discuss transversal  projects between each between all the  poles  and that is done in the presence of the  medical director and so he can bring  back the ideas to the general director  and to the directors committee and we  can go forward all together  so that's oh that's a pretty effective  way of managing  the medical activities of the hospital  great thanks again um dr kornreich for  that really  in-depth answer appreciate that okay and  our next question  what is the composition of the  scientific commission and  what do they actually advise in the lab  uh the composition of the scientific  commission i don't see  what you mean by that  monica could you maybe elaborate a  little bit on your question  perhaps um i think maybe she means who  all  is as you know in the maybe in the poll  or in the commission  um like what departments do they come  from  do you have i think that might be what  monica means let me move on to another  question and maybe that monica will  you know elaborate a little bit more the  next question  do you see a future in the eu where  health care may be handled the same way  across all countries or will countries  continue to maintain their own health  care approaches  well again i think this is a question  where  uh there is the utopia of  having maybe everything done the same  way everywhere in europe  but the reality of of things is that  all the countries in europe have  difference in living standards  and uh in demographic pressure  and medical services and technologies  and that would make it very  difficult to have a homogeneous way  of um of handling healthcare  uh throughout europe there are probably  what things what that we can do together  and i think  again the kovit pandemic showed us that  it's necessary  to get together for  infectious diseases which no no borders  but maybe for other healthcare issues  those still have to be handled within  each country  as an example in belgium we have a we  have nine  ministers for health care because  the country is so divided between  communities and regions and different  languages and  it shows how difficult it is within a  country to have a uniform way of  managing health care and doing that at  the european level  uh seems to me like something that would  be very difficult to achieve  yeah so much that comes into play there  right yes  okay next question here dr cornright  uh as you indicated more charges are  becoming the responsibility of the  patient  do you have a private insurance option  in belgium for people  to use to cover those expenses  okay so i i talked about the compulsory  health care insurance in belgium which  covers  most of the health care expenditure but  there are private insurance options  either proposed by the um  [Music]  the mutual um care  uh because there's a there's still a  level between the  federal reimbursement and the patient  which are the mutual funds so mutual  funds can offer complement  complementary additional  health care insurance private insurance  but there are also  [Music]  private insurance funds that can also  propose private insurance those  insurance usually cover  hospitalization and in their coverage  they usually cover  one month before hospitalization and  three months after  but they will uh control that uh the  expenditure that the patient had is  in um is in uh  um it is within  the pathology that was treated uh  during hospitalization so they won't  cover all  the outpatients costs  a specific insurance for outpatient  costs i don't think  they exist in belgium there are some  outpatient costs such as  certain medication or  yeah i think or maybe some  uh apparatus that the patient might need  but that usually goes through the  the expert doctor of the mutual fund  so you have to submit um a claim  to to get reimbursement uh which won't  be automatically covered by the  the compulsory um health insurance that  all veterans have  i hope that answers the question  okay great thank you again uh dr  kornreich  it looks like that's uh all that we have  time for today  do you have any final comments for our  audience today dr cornright  uh no i hope i hope you found the the  uh the talk interesting there are a lot  more information  on the slides than what i uh what i  explained  during the talk so i hope you you will  find  uh by rereading the slides of answers to  questions that maybe didn't have a  chance to  to ask during this q a and thank you for  attending  and thank you for your questions  well thank you again dr cornwright for  your time today  and for your important research we would  also like to thank labyrinth and our  sponsor abbott  for underwriting today's educational  webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today  and for their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information  you provided at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  routes will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  until next time goodbye


